@@ -0,0 +1,182 @@
+# Sessions, branching and live events
+
+## AgentSession lifecycle (`lib/rpc-manager.ts`)
+- One `AgentSessionWrapper` per session id in `globalThis.__piSessions` (`globalThis` survives Next.js hot reload; a module-level Map does not). Concurrent `startRpcSession()` calls share one start Promise (`globalThis.__piStartLocks`). Idle timeout: 10 minutes (`PI_WEB_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS`, `0` disables).
+- Stop cannot cancel a run awaiting a promise that ignores the abort signal (a third-party extension handler or tool): `inner.abort()` never returns. So Stop (`abort`, `abort_bash`) sets `forceShutdownOnIdle` and arms the idle timer, shutting the wrapper down one idle timeout after the first Stop even while it runs. Later commands (a reload's `get_tools`, Stop again) must not push that deadline back; with `PI_WEB_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=0` it still arms, at 10 minutes.
+- **A wrapper is closing from the moment `shutdown()` or `destroy()` starts**: `isAlive()` turns false at once, so routes stop sending it commands, but `startRpcSession()` builds the replacement only once it has disposed (`closingRpcSessionWait()`, at most the shutdown deadline plus 1 s). Until then it owns the session: an extension's `session_shutdown` may append to the file, and `dispose()` releases provider resources (a Codex websocket) by session id. `setRpcSessionTools()` waits likewise for a closing wrapper or a start under way, then applies the selection to the resulting wrapper, never through a second `SessionManager`. A stuck run counts as busy (`isRunning()`) until disposal.
+- `reload` is refused while the session runs (`isSessionRunningForReplacement()`, as `clone`): it invalidates the extension runner, and the running turn still holds the old `ctx`, so it died at its next hook with "This extension ctx is stale…" (#1172). pi's TUI refuses `/reload` while streaming or compacting too, with the same text. An extension command's `ctx.reload()` checks only streaming and compacting, as the TUI's does, and warns instead of throwing: the command runs inside its own `prompt`, so a pending prompt there is the command itself.
+- The closing wrapper stays in `__piSessions` until disposed or replaced; its `onDestroy` removes the entry only while it still points at that wrapper, or a late cleanup would unregister the replacement. `registerRpcWrapper()` clears the callback of the entry it replaces (a pre-hot-reload wrapper still deletes by id).
+- Extensions get `PI_WEB_SHUTDOWN_DEADLINE_MS` (5 s; `0` or invalid keeps the default) for `session_shutdown`, then the wrapper logs once and disposes anyway: closing an MCP connection has no upper bound. Extension binding is awaited first, without a deadline.
+- A session whose cwd was deleted outside pi-web still opens (its transcript, the event stream's wrapper), but `send("prompt")` refuses with `Directory does not exist: <cwd>`, the text `/api/agent/new` answers for a fresh composer's missing cwd (#1061): a run would reach the model only for its tools to fail, and pi's `write` would recreate the folder. The pi CLI does not resume such a session either. The composer shows the refusal as a notice and keeps the draft; `ensureNewSession()` passes the server's `error` on, never a bare `HTTP 400`.
+
+## Fork never touches the running AgentSession
+Never fork through `AgentSession.fork()` / `AgentSessionRuntime.fork()`: they replace the session in place (`inner.sessionId` becomes the new id; the current run is aborted first), so a wrapper registered under the old id would serve the forked state and corrupt the `parentSession` chain.
+
+`send("fork")` / `send("fork_branch")` open a separate `SessionManager` on the source file and `createBranchedSession()` from it. So forking works **while the source runs** (only a running `!` shell command refuses): pi appends each finished entry synchronously in this process, and the in-flight assistant message lands after the fork point. A source with no file yet is refused with the upstream "has not been saved yet" wording. After a `fork` (never `fork_branch`) an idle source is shut down, since the browser moves to the child; a running one keeps its run and its running row. `clone` refuses a running session: it copies the current branch, which is the run in progress, and then shuts the source down.
+
+The sidebar's Fork (row menu, F) is pi's `/clone` result (`fork(leaf, { position: "at" })`) done on disk with no runtime at all: `POST /api/sessions/[id]/fork` → `forkSessionBranch()` (`lib/session-fork.ts`). It never starts, prompts or shuts down a wrapper, nor touches its idle timer, so unlike `clone` it is allowed mid-run, and a running `!` command does not refuse it (its entry is not on disk yet). It copies the finished prefix of the branch, which can end on the run's prompt with no reply or on tool calls still waiting for results (pi-ai's `transformMessages` gives those a "No result provided" result when the copy is continued); the chat's `fork` cannot, since it cuts before a user message. The leaf is the one `GET /api/sessions/[id]` shows: an open wrapper's in-memory leaf (a navigated branch), else the file's last entry; an idle wrapper the file has moved past (another pi process appended) is ignored, as `?force=1` would evict it. `createBranchedSession()` repoints the instance it runs on, so it runs on a fresh `SessionManager.open()`, never on `openSessionManager()`'s cached reader or a wrapper's. Refusals: `not_found` (404; it also drops the cached path and bumps the list version, so a session deleted from the CLI leaves the tree), `unsaved` (no file yet, or the wrapper's leaf is not on disk), `empty` (no `message` entry on the branch; a shell-only branch is copied, written by hand as `persistBashOnlySession()` does, since pi writes a file only once it has a user or assistant message), `subagent` (valid subagent metadata on the branch: the copy would be folded, hidden, into the parent's family). pi's `session_before_fork` / `session_fork` extension hooks do not run, as for the chat's fork: no runtime is loaded. pi's open-time migration may rewrite an old-version source, as viewing it does; otherwise the source is untouched. Labels, compactions, model changes and the `session_info` names on the branch are copied as pi copies them.
+
+The copy then gets a name of its own, so the tree tells it from its source: the title the sidebar shows for the source, ` · `, and 4 random lowercase hex digits from `crypto` (`PR#1030 状态栏命令按钮 · 3f9a`; `lib/session-fork-name.ts`). That title is read as the list reads the source's row (`readForkSourceTitle()` → `scanSessionFileInfo()`: the newest `session_info` name in the whole file, else the first user message) through `sessionRowTitle()`'s rule (`sessionDisplayTitle()`: a skill expansion as its `/skill:` command, else the id; a test keeps the two equal), so a name given on another branch than the one copied still names the copy, as the source's row shows it. It is the route's one await before the copy: the leaf is read after it, with none between. A source with no title of its own (no name and no user text, shell-only or images only: the list's `(no messages)` placeholder) leaves the copy unnamed (`readForkSourceTitle()` → null), as pi's `/clone` does, so its row takes the first message its user writes in it, and no English placeholder is stored as a name; a blank title (a whitespace-only first message) takes the id rule, never a bare suffix. The title goes on one line; when the source is itself a fork (header `parentSession`), an earlier Fork's suffix at its end is dropped, so forks of forks do not stack suffixes, while a user's own "Weekly sync · 2024" stays whole; it is then cut to 40 code points ending in an ellipsis (never splitting a surrogate pair): a name, not a whole first message. The copy's row and the Fork's toast never cut the suffix, whatever the width (a code-point budget cannot do that, CJK being twice as wide): `splitForkSuffix()` gives a fork's row (`relation.kind === "fork"` with a name ending in a suffix) its title as two spans in `.session-tree-title.has-fork-suffix`, the ellipsis cutting the base before a suffix that does not shrink. The name is a `session_info` appended on the forking `SessionManager` once `createBranchedSession()` has repointed it (`appendSessionInfo()`, as PATCH's rename writes one): only the copy's file gets it, the source's bytes stay as they were, and a shell-only copy has it in the file written by hand. Only the sidebar's Fork names its copy: the chat's `fork` ("New session" on a message) and `clone` keep pi's behaviour, their copy titled by the newest name on the copied branch, else its first message.
+
+The answer carries the copy's catalogue row (`readSessionInfo()` once the name is written, the list's own shape, plus `relation: { kind: "fork", originSessionId }`), so the sidebar shows it, named, before the next list load and the row does not move when that load replaces it.
+
+## In-session branching waits for the run
+pi's `navigateTree()` refuses while streaming or compacting: the file has one leaf pointer that the run appends under, and navigating swaps the agent's context. So "Edit from here" is hidden while busy, the BranchNavigator renders read-only (`locked`) with a note, and `handleLeafChange` refuses (switching only the view would show the live run under another branch). To branch from a running session, fork it.
+
+## Two kinds of branching — don't confuse them
+- **Fork** ("New session" on a user message, or the sidebar's Fork): a new `.jsonl` file whose header `parentSession` names the source. It is its own row in its project group (`relation.kind: "fork"`); only subagents fold into a family, so copying a subagent session would carry its metadata and hide the copy in the parent's family. Rows sort by their last user/assistant message: a chat fork copies up to the parent of a user message, so it is older than its source and sorts below it (one made at the first message is header-only and sorts by its header time, first); a sidebar Fork of the newest branch ties with its source and sorts first (`[s, ...current]` on the client, newest-mtime-first on the server).
+- **In-session branch** ("Edit from here" / BranchNavigator): `navigate_tree` within one file; entries share a `parentId`. Switching calls `/api/sessions/[id]/context?leafId=`.
+
+## Session files can be fully rewritten
+`parentSession` in the header is **display metadata only**, so rewriting the whole file with `writeFileSync` is safe (pi does it in migrations); cascade-reparenting children on delete relies on this.
+
+## ToolCall field normalization
+Pi stores `{type:"toolCall", id, name, arguments}`; `ToolCallContent` uses `{toolCallId, toolName, input}`. `normalizeToolCalls()` (`lib/normalize.ts`) converts in both `session-reader.ts` (file load) and `handleAgentEvent` (`hooks/useAgentSession.ts`, streaming).
+
+## SSE reconnect on page refresh mid-stream
+On mount `useAgentSession` loads the history, then `GET /api/sessions/[id]/state` (`loadSession(…, includeState)`): `state.isStreaming` or `state.isPromptRunning` resumes the run in the UI, and `thinkingLevel` / `isCompacting` sync from it. No reconnect is needed: the selected session's stream stays open whether or not it runs (`maintainEventsConnected()`, warm-session effect), and its `connected` event carries `isStreaming`.
+
+## Loading older history (`components/ChatWindow.tsx`)
+The chat renders the last `visibleCount` rows, kept at least the loaded message count, with a sentinel above them while rows are hidden or the server has older entries (`hasEarlierMessages`, paged with `loadContext(…, before)`). Rows are not messages: a turn's answer and its thinking render as two, so loaded rows can outnumber the window; with nothing older on the server, reaching the sentinel widens the window (`getNextVisibleCount()`). The sentinel element is state (a callback ref), so the observer attaches whenever it appears, and the observer is renewed when the window grows: IntersectionObserver reports only changes, so a sentinel still in view would wait for the user to scroll away and back. A page lands, and `loadingOlderRef` clears, a moment before the next cursor renders; a report in between still holds the old cursor and prepended the same page twice. So `loadOlderPage()` keeps the cursor it asked for until another one renders, and the observer skips it; a page that failed, or a cursor a reload brings back, can be asked for again.
+- The minimap numbers turns from the branch's first, not the loaded page's (#791). Each context page carries `turnsBefore`: the turn anchors on the branch above its oldest entry, counted with `isMessageGroupAnchor()` on the shape `entryToUiMessage()` would give (`countTurnsBefore()`, `lib/session-reader.ts`), so the client's turns and the server's count agree. The hook keeps it beside the history cursor (`earlierTurnCount`, also in the view cache); a loaded turn is numbered `turnsBefore + index + 1` and keeps that number as older pages land. While it is above 0 the rail shows a dotted stub above the first node and the preview's first row says how many turns are not loaded; clicking it scrolls the chat to the top, so the sentinel pages them in. Unloaded turns have no placeholder nodes yet.
+
+## Compaction SSE events
+`handleAgentEvent` accepts `compaction_start` / `compaction_end` and the older `auto_compaction_*` pair to keep `isCompacting` in sync. Manual compact is a blocking POST: `isCompacting` holds until it returns, the button meanwhile Stop compaction (`abort_compaction`). When a run ends, Compact (and the tools button) take the spot Stop held left of the sound toggle, so `ChatInput` ignores pointer clicks on them for `RUN_END_CLICK_GUARD_MS` (600 ms) after it: the second click of a double click on Stop otherwise started a compaction nobody confirmed (#1131). Keyboard activation (`detail` 0) and Stop compaction are never held back.
+
+## Process details start open without an answer (`ProcessDetailsGroup`, `components/ChatWindow.tsx`)
+A grouped turn's Process details start collapsed only when the answer shown under them has text or an image (`collapsesProcessDetails()`, `lib/message-display.ts`). A turn that ends on an error or truncation notice alone, e.g. empty `stopReason: "error"` replies after text and a trailing tool call, still shows that notice but keeps them open: the earlier text is inside them (#906). The group is keyed on this decision, so a leaf switch that changes it resets the group; a search hit inside opens it (`reveal`).
+
+## Compaction summary card (`CompactionMessageView`, `components/MessageView.tsx`)
+Collapsed by default, as pi's TUI `CompactionSummaryMessageComponent` (#1026): the header, "Conversation compacted" and pi's "Compacted from N tokens" (`details.tokensBefore`, set by the reader). The title is the toggle (a button with `aria-expanded`); the description, the summary Markdown and the file lists mount only when expanded. Session search indexes only user and assistant `message` entries (`lib/session-search.ts`), so no hit lands inside a summary and the card needs no `reveal` like Process details.
+
+## Streaming token rate
+The streaming reply's `t/s` counts estimated tokens from when its first ones showed. That start lives in `lib/stream-token-rate.ts`, keyed by the reply (provider, model and request `timestamp`), not in `MessageView`: switching sessions remounts the chat, and a start taken at the remount divided every token streamed so far by a fraction of a second. A reply joined with no record (a reload) counts only the tokens that follow.
+
+## Auto-retry banner
+`auto_retry_start` shows "Retrying (n/max)…" above the composer for the backoff wait. The retried run's `agent_start` clears it, as pi's TUI replaces its retry indicator at that run's `turn_start`: pi emits a successful `auto_retry_end` only with the retry's first complete assistant message, minutes into a long reply. `auto_retry_end`, `agent_end` and settling clear it too.
+
+## Tool execution events on the SSE stream
+- Calls made through `ctx.executeTool()` (a codemode script's) emit `tool_execution_*` with `parentToolCallId` and ids `<parent>/<n>`. `toClientAgentEvent()` sends their start and end slim and drops their updates; test for nesting *before* rebuilding an update, which keeps only `toolCallId`, `toolName` and `partialResult` (a nested update would reach the browser as a top-level tool). `handleAgentEvent` keeps them out of the running-tools phase; `AgentSessionWrapper` never records them for replay, a parent's end forgets every id under `<parent>/`, and `agent_end` clears the replay set.
+- `tool_execution_end` never carries `result` (up to 1 MiB of bash output): the browser renders the tool result message that follows. A non-assistant `message_start` (that tool result, a delivered user message) goes as `{ role }` only: the loop sends it right before its `message_end`, and the browser builds only an assistant reply from a start, so an image read's base64 went out twice. `entry_appended` is omitted. A codemode update's `details.calls` keeps the newest 200, `omittedCalls` counting the rest.
+- `createAgentEventStream()` coalesces `tool_execution_update` per `toolCallId` (latest wins, `TOOL_UPDATE_COALESCE_MS`) and discards that id's pending update before forwarding its `tool_execution_end` (`agent_end` discards all), or the tool would read as running again. Everything else goes at once, so a pending update can arrive after unrelated events.
+- A codemode card shows the script in the box any tool's input uses (plain text) and its `details.calls` as rows, never as cards: those calls never reach the model as tool calls. `handleAgentEvent` keeps a running script's progress snapshot in `activeToolResults`, as for shell output, so calls list while they run.
+- A tool card's time is the result's `durationMs` (pi 1.1: `execute()` measured with a monotonic clock, saved in the session file), formatted as the CLI's "Took" (`formatToolDuration()`), and none under 100 ms. An older result falls back to its timestamp minus the assistant message's, which is when that message's request **started**, so it counts the model's generation too. A thinking block's time likewise prefers the assistant message's own `durationMs`.
+
+## Custom messages collapse from their header (`CustomMessageView`, `components/MessageView.tsx`)
+A displayed custom message starts expanded: pi's TUI draws its default custom message in full and has no per-message toggle (only a `registerMessageRenderer` renderer gets the global Ctrl+O `expanded` flag, and pi-web runs no TUI renderers). The header is a button (`aria-expanded`, the tool cards' chevron as its **last** element, the timestamp just before it) that folds the card to that one row, with the text's start on it as a one-line preview, like a tool card's header; the body, the footer (Copy, details) and the details mount only when expanded. The footer button is about `details` only. Session search never lands in a custom message (it indexes user and assistant entries), so the card needs no `reveal`.
+
+## Transcript system messages, usage entries and context edits (pi >= 0.86)
+- A new session's first request persists a `message` entry with `role: "system"` (prompt sections, tool declarations); prompt or tool changes append more, announced with `message_start` / `message_end`. They are provider input, never conversation: `toClientAgentEvent()` drops them (they carry every tool schema), `handleAgentEvent` skips strays, `entryToUiMessage()` returns null, and `BranchNavigator` / `lib/project-tree.ts` never label or preview a branch with one. They still count toward `messageCount` and `totalMessages`, as in the SDK.
+- `usage` entries (`kind: "cache_warm"`): billed prompt-cache warming outside model context; `computeSessionStats()` adds them like compaction usage so counters match the TUI's `/session`.
+- `context_edit` entries omit or replace an earlier entry's model context without changing raw history; the UI ignores them. A retain-none compaction stores its own id in `firstKeptEntryId`.
+- `listSessionsIncremental()` must keep `SessionManager.listAll()`'s order: newest mtime first, then reverse filename, stable for equal activity time.
+
+## Turn grouping and custom messages in the chat (`ChatWindow`, `lib/message-display.ts`)
+- A turn runs from one anchor (`isMessageGroupAnchor()`) to the next; its last assistant answer shows, everything before it folds into Process details. Anchors: user messages, the compaction summary, and background subagent completions, which arrive as a follow-up that triggers a new run after the answer: pi-web's `pi-web:subagent-notification` and `@tintinweb/pi-subagents`' bare `subagent-notification` (#891, #1075). Without the anchor, the reply to the notification becomes the turn's answer and the real one folds away.
+- A custom message without `display` (`display: false` keeps it for the model alone) stays in `messages` and `entryIds`, from the reader and from `message_end`, but renders nothing, as in pi's TUI: `isHiddenCustomMessage()` makes `renderMessage()` return null, keeps it out of the Process details count, and it never anchors a turn. An extension may send one on every `session_start`, i.e. on each idle restart (#1043).
+
+## Running state polling + reconciliation
+- The sidebar polls `/api/agent/running` every 2.5 s while the tab is visible; the session-list response is the initial fallback.
+- The same poll carries `awaitingInputSessionIds`: the running sessions with an unanswered blocking `extension_ui_request` (`isBlockingExtensionUiRequest()`, `lib/extension-ui-queue.ts` — select / confirm / input / editor, or a custom overlay that has not closed), as `AgentSessionWrapper.isAwaitingInput()` reads `pendingUiRequests`. The row shows that in the spinner's slot as an amber breathing dot (the unread dot's shape and animation) and stays a running row everywhere else: same `is-running` class, no archive button, group and pinned-header counts unchanged, the archive predicate untouched. The wrapper checks `isRunning()` first, so a custom overlay that outlives its turn is not a parked run. `/api/agent/running` is the only source: a background session's own SSE stream has no listener, which is why the browser notification (`#431`'s `handleAttentionNeeded`) cannot cover this.
+- The list's `firstMessage` is a row title, not the prompt: `catalogFirstMessage()` sends a skill expansion as its `/skill:` command and cuts the rest to 500 code units (persisted rows and live ones from `getRpcSessionInfos()`). Whole first messages made the list 3.2 MB (one row 325 KB), fetched again by every tab after each run. Fork naming reads the file itself and is unaffected.
+- A run's end costs one list rebuild: the wrapper invalidates the list at `agent_end`, before the event reaches any browser, so nothing after it forces one. The tab showing the session reloads through `refreshKey` (`handleAgentEnd`; the reload's row reaches the selected session through `handleSessionsChange` → `mergeCatalogRow()`, which also clears a fresh session's `transient`), the others through the version poll and the background-completion reload, all plain `loadSessions()`. A `force=1` there rescanned again and bumped the version, so each tab not showing the session made every other tab reload once more (5 list requests and 3 rebuilds per run with two tabs). Only the first page load's details hydration still forces, to pick up what the pi CLI appended meanwhile.
+- `invalidateSessionListCache()` bumps the generation but **keeps** the previous scan, fresh only while its generation matches. Callers needing only metadata (search hits to sidebar rows) pass `listAllSessions({ allowStale: true })` to read it while it rebuilds in the background, accepting that a seconds-old session is missing.
+- `useAgentSession` treats per-session SSE as primary and opens it before each prompt. `prompt_done` completes the UI stage and notification at once (none for a stopped run: `prompt_done.aborted`, see **Completion sound** in [client-platform.md](client-platform.md)), but the stream stays open for the next prompt: the selected session's while selected (`scheduleEventStreamClose()` skips it), any other's for a 30-second grace window. `agent_start` cancels the close timer; `agent_settled` finishes extension-injected runs that have no wrapper-level `prompt_done` and starts a fresh grace window. Never close on the first `agent_end`: retries, compaction and extension-queued messages continue the same logical prompt.
+- While a run is active, `useAgentSession` polls `GET /api/agent/[id]` and reconciles on `visibilitychange` / `online`, for terminal events missed by background tabs or half-open connections. Busy replies also refresh context usage; each completed assistant message requests an immediate usage read between model calls, and so does each `connected`: opening the stream is what resumes an idle-reaped wrapper, so the mount's state read can find no runtime and no usage. Usage reads from mount, stream connect, message completion, reconciliation and `agent_end` share a monotonic request id and check the current session/run and mounted hook. A reply applies only when its id is newer than the last applied one, so a delayed read cannot overwrite newer usage, and a newer read that fails does not discard an older one still in flight. Each mount marks the reads started before it as stale.
+- A fresh composer's first send outlives the composer: switching sessions while it runs unmounts the hook and closes its stream, but `handleSend` still posts the prompt (skipping the stream wait once unmounted) and promotes the session, so it runs and gets its sidebar row. Before #1146 a switch during the `connected` handshake rejected the wait, and `restoreSubmission` drops an abandoned composer's draft, so the text was lost.
+- Prompt runs carry a monotonic run id; late SSE or reconciliation answers from an old run must be ignored, or they resurrect stale streaming bubbles.
+- Every SSE (re)connection is gated on `sessionHookMountedRef`. Under React Strict Mode (`next dev`) the mount-only effect's cleanup clears it and restores it only after the warm-session effect re-runs, so that effect must re-assert it before `maintainEventsConnected()`, or a dev tab never opens its stream.
+
+## Session sidebar: project groups, pins and archive
+State (`lib/session-ui-state.ts`, `lib/session-ui-state-shared.ts`, `app/api/sessions/ui-state/route.ts`, `hooks/useSessionUiState.ts`):
+- Pins, archive times, the project order and project display names are pi-web UI state in `<agentDir>/pi-web-session-state.json` (`{ version: 1, revision, sessions: { <family root id>: { pinnedAt?, archivedAt? } }, projects: { <projectKey>: { pinnedAt, root } }, projectOrder?: [<projectKey>, …], projectNames?: { <projectKey>: name } }`, the order top first, the order and the names absent while empty), **never in a `.jsonl`**. A custom entry would advance the leaf (a phantom branch), make an idle wrapper's `evictIfDiskAhead()` drop it, and be copied by every fork; moving files into an archive folder breaks the children's absolute `parentSession` paths; localStorage would keep them in one browser while one server serves several. So the pi CLI sees none of it: its `/resume` still lists archived sessions.
+- Flags are keyed by the family root id (subagent sessions fold into their root's row); projects by `workspaceKeyOf()`, with the root kept so a pinned project with no live sessions still has a path.
+- `POST /api/sessions/ui-state` takes one request: `set` (`ids` + exactly one of `pinned` / `archived`, at most 500 ids), `restore` (exact prior values, `null` = absent: Undo), `pin-project`, `add-projects` (`keys`), `move-project` (`projectKey`, `anchorKey`, `position: "before" | "after"`, `add`), `rename-project` (`projectKey`, `name: string | null`). A bigger change (a bulk archive and its Undo) goes in parts of 500 (`chunkForSessionUiRequests()`), queued in order; a refused part rolls back alone. Ids are not checked against the session list; an orphan is harmless. `applySessionUiStateRequest()` is shared so the client applies the change optimistically exactly as the server will: pin and archive exclude each other (pinning an archived family restores it; archiving unpins), archiving again refreshes `archivedAt` (so a family that came back can be archived again), pinning an already pinned family keeps its `pinnedAt`, an entry left empty is deleted. Timestamps are the server's `Date.now()`; the hook replays queued writes with the click time only for its local view.
+- `projectOrder` is one list for both bands (pinned projects, the others); each band shows its own keys in list order. Nobody writes the whole list, so several windows never lose each other's changes: `add-projects` puts the keys the list lacks in front, in their order (idempotent: two windows saving the same new projects write once); `move-project` first adds `add` the same way, then places `projectKey` right before or after `anchorKey` (a missing anchor goes to the top first, where an unsaved project renders). A key not shown (an archived-only project, an old key after `git init`) keeps its place, so a project that comes back takes its old slot; a neighbour hidden between two shown keys does not change what a before/after move shows. Limits: 1000 keys and 256 KiB as UTF-8 JSON (`PROJECT_ORDER_MAX_BYTES`; a 4096-character key of CJK text is 12 KiB, a Windows `\` doubles, and a file over its 4 MiB read limit would take pins and archive down with it). `add-projects` never pushes a key out: what does not fit is not stored (the last keys that fit go in, so the unsaved ones keep rendering above them), and the sidebar stops asking once the list is full; only `move-project` evicts, from the end, keys it does not name. `pin-project` moves the key when its band changes, while a list exists: a pin to the bottom of the pinned band (after the last pinned key), an unpin to the top of the others (before the first key not pinned); a new root for a pinned project moves nothing, and a full list that lacks the key stays as it is. None of these use `now`, so the hook's replay matches the server. A malformed `projectOrder` reads as none; it is no reason to set the file aside.
+- `projectNames` (#1047) is a label only, keyed by projectKey like the pins: the root, the key, the order, pins, expansion and sessions stay as they are, and nothing reaches the project's folder or the pi CLI. `projectDisplayName()` is the one rule for requests, the optimistic apply and the file: trimmed, one line (no C0/C1 controls, U+2028/2029), at most 80 code points (the field's `maxLength` counts UTF-16 units, so it never lets more through); empty or `null` clears. Duplicate names are allowed (the path tooltip tells them apart). Limits as for the order (1000 names, 256 KiB as UTF-8 JSON); a new name that does not fit is not stored, never pushing another out, and the route answers 409 `full` instead of a state that quietly lacks it. A malformed `projectNames` reads as none. The group menu offers Rename… (the header turns into the session row's `RenameInput`: Enter or blur saves, Escape cancels; the header's toggle takes focus back if it fell to the page) and Reset name while one is set; `projectRenameRequest()` saves nothing for an untouched field and clears for an empty one or the folder's own name. `SidebarProject.name` is the display name (`customName` the stored one), so group headers, the drag ghost, pinned rows' project label and the archive's group headers follow; the pickers get it as `ProjectChoice.alias` (`mergeProjectChoices(…, names)`, `withProjectAlias()`, part of `newSessionContextKey()`): the box shows the name instead of the path, in the path's code type; the menus show the name alone, no note (in code type on a desktop); the full path stays the tooltip, so the filter matches the name, the folder and the path.
+- `revision` is bumped only when a write changes something. Writes go through `serializeByKey()` in-process, then `proper-lockfile` across processes (about 3 s of retries, then 409 `locked`; `onCompromised` only warns, the default would throw from a timer and take the server down), then `writePrivateFileAtomicSync()` (0600). Unknown top-level fields of a valid file are kept. An unparsable file reads as empty and is never overwritten: the next write renames it to `.corrupt-<ms>` and starts empty with the revision still moving forward (the highest revision seen is kept per path), so clients notice.
+- `GET /api/agent/running` carries `sessionUiStateRevision` (one `statSync` while the file is unchanged: reads are cached by mtime, size and inode); the sidebar's poll passes it to `noteRevision()`, which refetches when it differs and no write is queued. That is how a pin or archive made in another browser arrives. `useSessionUiState` keeps the last server state with every queued write replayed on top, runs writes one at a time in call order (archive then Undo stays ordered), ignores a GET older than a later read or write, and on a refused write refetches (rollback) and the sidebar shows `sidebar.uiStateFailed`. The tree shows "loading" until the first GET settles (`loaded`, failed or not), so archived rows never flash in; `synced` turns true only once server state was adopted (a GET or a write response) and never back, and what the sidebar saves on its own waits for it. `hooks/useSessionUiState.test.mjs` plays this out against a scripted `fetch`; it and `hooks/useGroupDrag.test.mjs` render through `hooks/__fixtures__/react-hook-shim.mjs`, a stand-in for React's hooks that jiti aliases as `react`.
+- Deleting (`deleteSessions()`, `lib/session-delete.ts`, behind `DELETE /api/sessions/[id]` and `POST /api/sessions/delete`) calls `forgetSessionUiState()` for every deleted id (best effort, no lock or file when none had an entry).
+
+Archived predicate (`isFamilyArchived()`, `lib/session-tree.ts`):
+- `archivedAt` set, no family member running, and `archivedAt >= Date.parse(root.modified)`. `root.modified` is the last user/assistant message, so a new message brings the family back by itself, including one sent from the pi CLI (`pi --resume`). Never use `family.latestModified`: subagent activity alone must not unarchive. A `detailsPending` summary row stays archived (its time is only the file's mtime, which a rename or custom entry also moves); an unparsable `modified` likewise.
+- A running family always shows (and the row's archive action is disabled while it runs: it would come straight back). Pinned = `pinnedAt` set and not archived.
+- Archiving clears the family's unread markers; Undo restores the snapshot and the markers, and so does a refused archive write, except on the session open by then. Unread stays per browser (`pi-web:unread-session-ids` in localStorage), as do the tab, group expand choices, the pinned section's collapse, the files section's fold and the files' ignored-files switch (`lib/sidebar-prefs.ts`). Those five are restored in a mount effect, never in a state initializer: the server renders the defaults, and a saved Files tab read during the first client render is a hydration mismatch. The layout's choice and the files section's height have their own hooks (`useFilesPlacement`, `useResizablePanel`), which also render the defaults first. Group choices are in `pi-web:sidebar-groups-v2`. The old `pi-web:sidebar-groups` also held every group the old keep-open had saved as open, which no entry tells from the user's own, so a browser without v2 takes only its collapses: `loadGroupExpansion()` writes them to v2 at once (an empty v2 too, so the old key is never read again), then `forgetRetiredSidebarKeys()` drops the old key; explicit expansions go back to the defaults once.
+
+Tree model (`buildSessionTree()`, pure; `components/SessionTree.tsx` virtualizes its flat `rows` over per-kind heights):
+- Pinned section first: every project's pinned families, newest pin first, 8 shown (+ show more, as for groups). Then one collapsible group per project: projects with live non-pinned families, the current project (even empty), and pinned projects. Pinned projects come first, then the rest; each band follows `projectOrder`, and activity (a new message, a run, unread, the selection) never moves a project that has a place in it. Projects without a place come first in their band, in the old order (pinned ones by pin time, the others by newest family activity, the current project with no live family first): those not saved on their own (the current project while it has no live family, a project of transient sessions only) before the rest, so saving the rest changes nothing on screen. The model lists them per band (`unorderedKeysByBand`) and the ones to save (`projectKeysToRecord`: pinned projects, also without sessions, and projects with a live family that has a file). The sidebar sends those with `add-projects` once its ui-state is `synced` and the full session details are in (the first paint's summary rows carry file mtimes): the first save records the activity order the user sees, and a new project lands at the top of its band and stays there until moved. More keys than one request holds (500; a long-time user's daily `~/pi-cwd/YYYYMMDD` folders are each a project) go bottom first (`nextProjectKeysToRecord()`): each batch lands where it shows, right below the keys still unsaved; top first, the first batch would drop below the rest and the next one would store that scrambled order for good. A key sent but still unsaved (refused, or no room left) ends the batch, since a key saved above it would drop below it. Each key goes out once per page (`recordedOrderKeysRef`, marked only when sent; a refused save is not retried in a loop), through the raw apply, so a failed background save shows no toast. A current project that has a place keeps it while empty, so after "Open another project…" its group may sit lower down. A group is open by default when current or pinned; an explicit choice (a toggle, Alt+click, collapse others, expand all, a fork's reveal) wins and is saved. When another project becomes current (a session picked in its group), the group that was open only as the current one stays open for the page's life (`keepOutgoingGroupOpen()` into `keptOpenGroups`, page memory), so it does not fold up and move the clicked row from under the pointer. It is never saved: saved, every project ever visited came back open after a reload and the tree only grew. The tree, the toggles and the group menu's disabled states read `effectiveGroupExpansion()` (the saved choices over the kept ones); every write goes through `saveGroupChoices()`, which saves explicit choices only and drops the kept entries they cover (all of them for a choice made for every group). After a reload only the current project, pinned projects and groups opened on purpose are open. Each shows its 6 newest families plus any later one that is running, unread or selected, then "show more · N" (N still hidden). A click reveals `SHOW_MORE_STEP` (20) more, never the whole project: a project with hundreds of sessions would otherwise mount and scroll them all at once. Families that show anyway (running, unread, selected) do not use up a click, so each one adds exactly 20 rows or what is left. That is decided at the click, by position: the more row offers `nextShown` (the window past the base limit that 20 more hidden families need, counted with today's flags), `handleShowMore()` stores it, and the tree then shows every family before that position plus the flagged ones after it. A family that turns selected, unread or running inside the window keeps its place and frees nothing; counted as a budget on every render, picking the last revealed row (now selected, so it showed "anyway") let the next, older family in below it. Once a click has revealed rows, "show less" sits beside it and folds back to the base limit (it is not offered when folding would hide nothing); the more row is then scrolled back into view, since the list can shrink by hundreds of pixels under the pointer, as it is after a keyboard "show more" (the focused button moves 20 rows down). A keyboard user's focus moves to the button that remains, else to the group or pinned header; it waits in a ref until a commit has that target mounted. The counts live in `moreShown` (per project key, `PINNED_MORE_KEY` for the pinned section; session state, not saved). Folding drops a window: every explicit choice that folds a group (a toggle, Alt+click, collapse others) goes through `saveGroupChoices()`, which forgets the folded groups' counts (`forgetShownMoreOfFolded()`), and folding the pinned section forgets its own, so a group opened again shows its first rows (and the running, unread or selected ones) only. A collapsed group shows running/unread counts. Footer: "Open another project…" and "Archived · N" (the archive view, `buildArchiveRows()`, grouped by project, newest `archivedAt` first).
+- The look keeps a few rules; new rows follow them. **Three text colors**: content (session titles) `--text`, labels (project names, section labels) `--text-muted`, meta (times, counts, branches, a pinned row's project, "show more", the footer) `--text-dim`; no `color-mix` greys of their own (`SessionTree.test.mjs` lists every `color:` in `app/sidebar.css`: besides those three only the accent, the error red and the delete button's white). **No icons in the list**: no folder per project (it read as a second file tree, and the files section's header as one more project) and no pin before the pinned section; the only icons are the chevrons, a pinned project's 10px pin and the activity summary after its name, the branch icon in a row's branch, and the footer's two. **Two alignment lines**: the chevron column (12px, at the rows' left inset plus a toggle's 5px: 11px in; the inset is 6px in every browser, a classic scrollbar taking its room from the right, so the brand, the files section's header, the picker's paths and the file tree keep the same two lines) and the text column 20px in from the inset (26px) (5px + the 12px chevron + the toggle's 3px gap), where project names, section labels, session titles (`padding-left: 20px` in their inset box), "show more", the empty group's note (inset + 20px), the archive's project names and the footer's labels start (the footer's 12px icons sit in the chevron column); `SessionTree.test.mjs` adds it up from the CSS. **The accent is a state's, never a label's**: the selected row's bar, a run's spinner (no accent folder for the current project).
+- Headers are section headers over their rows, not one more row: the chevron leads (`ChevronIcon size={12} strokeWidth={3}`: a 1.5px stroke, as firm as a bold label; `--text-dim`, `--text-muted` on the header's hover; inside the toggle and `aria-hidden`, so the whole header still toggles and its name is the project's), then the name 12px/600 in `--text-muted` (`--text` for the current project, which shows by that alone, or a hovered one), then a pinned project's pin (5px off the name) and, folded, the activity summary at the right. The pinned section's header is a section label as tall as a group's (28px, 44px on a phone, a session row's height): its chevron in the same column, "Pinned" 11px/600 in small capitals (`letter-spacing: 0.05em`, uppercase; `--text` on hover), the count " · N" 11px/400 dim, then the dots while folded; the files section's header below the sessions is the same label. The archive view's project headers fold nothing: no chevron, the name 12px/600 muted in the text column; as tall as a group's header, the name at the bottom of its row (`align-items: flex-end`, 3px under it, at a phone's 44px too) since no spacer row parts the projects there. Phones take 13px for titles, project names and the archive's names (`.session-tree.is-mobile`, the component's layout prop, never a media query). The drag ghost's name is 12px/600, as a header's.
+- Spacing: groups sit right on each other, open or folded (collapsed ones at a 28px pitch); a gap between two of them only pushed the next header away. An 8px spacer row parts the pinned section from the groups (`pinned-spacer`) and the groups from the footer (`footer-spacer`, only when there are groups; a pinned section with no group has the one spacer before the footer).
+- Heights (`SIDEBAR_ROW_HEIGHTS`) keep one rhythm: session rows, every header (a project's, the pinned section's, the archive's projects), the empty group's note and the footer's two links are 28px (44px on a phone, a finger's minimum, headers included); only "show more" is a step shorter (26px, 36px). Every control in a row fits it with 2px to spare (⋯ and archive, the rename field and a delete confirmation's buttons 24px, a header's + and ⋯ 22px; 32px on a phone; `SessionTree.test.mjs` checks it), and the drag ghost (22px) stays shorter than a header.
+- Rows: nothing before the title; the right column shows the awaiting-input dot (amber, breathing), else the running spinner, else the unread dot, else the short time (a pinned row's project, an archived row's archive time). A running row keeps its spinner beside ⋯ on hover. Hover and selection are a rounded box inset from both edges; titles are 12px `--text` (the file tree's size), the selected one 500, and the selected row (in the archive view too) has a 3px accent bar on its box's left edge, 7px clear of its top and bottom (a 14px bar; 12px on a phone, 20px), a `::before` of the absolutely positioned row, so its rounded corners never clip it; a delete confirmation's red frame has none. A worktree's branch is meta in the rows' own type (11px dim, a 10px `BranchIcon` kept inline so the ellipsis still cuts the name; the tooltip keeps `⑂`), as a pinned row's project is (no code type). A classic scrollbar takes width on the right only (Chromium's and Safari's styled one is 10px, 6px on a coarse pointer; an overlay one none): `SessionTree` measures it into `--session-tree-scrollbar`, and the right inset shrinks by it, so rows end 6px in or on the gutter's edge, whichever is further in, while the left inset stays 6px (moving it too put the tree's two lines 4px right of everything else's in Chromium). It also puts the width on the sidebar's root (only a tree with a box: not one under the archive view or in a hidden tab), where the files below the sessions read it for their right edge. A row's time (or spinner, dot) ends 4px inside its box, where a folded group's summary ends: neither the row nor its always-rendered actions span has a gap, each shown action brings its own 2px (`margin-left`), so the hidden ones leave the time on the edge. The list keeps the scrollbar's room even when everything fits (`scrollbar-gutter: stable`; `overflow-y: scroll` in Safari before 18.2), so rows never narrow when it starts to scroll.
+- Row keys are unique and stable (`session:<context>:<rootId>`, `group:<key>`, `more:<key>`, `empty:<key>`, and fixed keys for the pinned header, the two spacers (`pinned-spacer`, `footer-spacer`) and the footer); focus tracking and `keepMounted` go by them.
+- Menus (`SidebarMenu`) and the toast live in `SessionSidebar`, above the virtualized rows, so a row scrolled away keeps its open menu (the project/worktree picker of the files tab and of the composer's bar uses `SidebarMenu` for its own two menus); the row with focus, a menu, a rename or a delete confirmation stays mounted (`keepMounted`). Menus are portaled to `document.body`: the mobile drawer's `transform` would pin a `position: fixed` menu to the drawer. The archive view is a second tree; the main one stays mounted underneath, the two in one grid cell (`.sidebar-sessions-views`, `grid-area: 1 / 1`; the archive opaque in `--bg-panel` and stacked above the tree for good, `position: relative; z-index: 1`: the tree's rows are positioned and paint over an unpositioned view, which only its animation lifted, so the frame its entrance ended in flashed the tree). Going in and out moves, in both layouts and on phones (`useSidebarMotion()`, `archiveViewState()` in `hooks/useSidebarMotion.ts`; `setArchiveView()` starts it on a change only, and View, Back and a fork's open all go through it): in, the archive slides from 14px right and fades in over the tree (`.is-entering`, 180ms ease-out), the tree `inert` under it until the view is in, then `hidden` as before; out, the tree is back at once and the archive slides and fades out to the right over it (`.is-leaving`, 180ms ease-in, `forwards`), still mounted with its rows (`archiveRows` is built while `archiveMounted`, else it would leave empty), `inert` and `pointer-events: none`, so nothing in it is clicked twice and a click lands on the tree, until its own `animationend` (not one bubbling from a row) unmounts it. A phase that gets no end event (a sidebar not shown: a hidden tab, a closed drawer) ends 300 ms after its start (`SIDEBAR_MOTION_MS` + `SIDEBAR_MOTION_GRACE_MS`). Under `prefers-reduced-motion` no phase starts and the CSS has no animation: the views swap at once, as before.
+- Focus: a menu returns it to the opener it was given when it opened (the parent drops `returnFocusTo` with the menu); a row's ⋯ or a group's buttons are `display: none` by then unless hovered, selected or focused, so `menuFocusReturnTarget()` falls back to the row's first focusable control (its main link or transient-session button, a group's toggle). Controls that go away under the keyboard hand focus on: the archive's Back button when it opens, the footer link (or the selected tab, or with no tabs the toolbar row's search) after Back, the family's row after a toast's Undo or a delete Cancel (`focusIfLost()`, only when focus fell to `<body>`). The archive's two moves make the view they leave `inert` before it is hidden or gone, and Chromium blurs an inert element only at its next frame, after the effect that moves focus has run: `focusIfHidden()` counts focus on something without a box or inside `[inert]` as lost too.
+- The running poll keeps the previous `Set` while the ids are unchanged (`sameIdsOr()`): tree rows are memoized by identity, and a new Set every 2.5 s would rebuild them all.
+- Toggling a group (Alt+click: every group follows it), show more and the group ⋯ never change the cwd. Only "Open in Files" in a group menu switches the project on purpose (and shows the files: `revealFiles()`); row clicks go through `handleSelectSessionFromList()`, which moves the cwd to the session's worktree.
+- Fork (F) is offered in the group, pinned and archive views, also while the family runs (`sessionMenuEntries()`). One per session at a time (`forkingIdsRef`): F again on a session whose fork is under way does nothing, another session's Fork goes ahead. The answer goes through `openForkedRef`, never a click-time closure: `openForked()` drops the main tree's saved scroll and closes the archive view (the copy has no flags; pins and archive are never inherited), opens the copy's group if the user collapsed it (re-inserted as the newest choice), selects it through `handleSelectSessionFromList()` (as a row click: cwd to its worktree, AppShell adopts the project), loads the list without `force` (the route already invalidated it), and asks the main tree to reveal `session:group:<id>`; a toast says "Forked “…”" with the copy's name, its ellipsis cutting only what precedes the suffix: `splitBeforeForkSuffix()` passes the suffix and the closing quote as the toast's `tail` (`SidebarToastData.tail`, a span that does not shrink); an unnamed copy is cut as other toasts are (`shortTitle()`). On a phone the drawer stays open for that pick (`SelectSessionOptions.keepSidebarOpen`, honoured by `AppShell.handleSelectSession`): the copy has its source's transcript and, but for the suffix, its title, so only the revealed row and the toast, both in the drawer, show that it happened. If the selection changed during the request, the user stays put: the copy is inserted, marked unread (so it shows beyond its group's limit) and the toast offers Open, which opens it as a row click does (the drawer closes). Not `AppShell.handleSessionForked`: its `prev` spread assumes the source is the selected session. A refusal toasts `forkFailureMessage()`'s text; `not_found` reloads the list.
+- Delete all sessions… is the group ⋯ menu's last item, apart and red, with its count (`familiesToDelete()`, live; disabled at 0): every family of the project that has a file, pinned and archived ones too (Archive hides sessions; this clears a project out), never one that runs or waits for input (deleting would stop the run) nor a transient one. The menu turns into a question (`askDelete` on the menu state; a custom body, `.sidebar-menu-question` in app/sidebar-menu.css): what goes, how many running ones are kept, "Delete N" and Cancel, which takes the focus on a desktop and goes back to the list (the sheet's own Cancel on a phone, `focusCancel`), so Enter never deletes. Delete closes the menu and sends the root ids counted at that moment (`deleteProjectSessions()`, `lib/sidebar-actions.ts`: parts of 500, one request at a time, a refused part ends the run); one run per project at a time (`deletingProjectsRef`). A toast says "Deleting sessions…", then how many went and how many runs were kept, or why a part failed (the parts before it are gone). Every deleted id goes to `onSessionDeleted`, so an open session among them falls back to a fresh composer; the list reloads, and focus that fell to the page with a vanished group goes to the tab. The project's pin, place, name and expansion are untouched: a current or pinned project keeps its group, empty. The route runs `deleteSessions(ids, { keepRunning: true })`: one catalogue listing and one read of each folder for the whole part (a request per session would read every file of the folder once per session), subagent sessions with their parent, children shut down before parents; a session that started running since the question is kept and reported (`running`), an id already gone is reported (`missing`). What stays is re-pointed at its nearest ancestor that stays, past any number of deleted ones; the row's Delete is the same code with one id.
+- Moving a group: drag its header (`hooks/useGroupDrag.ts`), or Move up / Move down in its ⋯ menu (disabled at the band's edges; `adjacentProjectMove()` on the project as the tree has it now, which may have been pinned elsewhere while the menu was open). Both call `moveProject()`, which refuses a move across bands, sends `move-project` with the band's `unorderedKeysByBand` (only that band's: a project dropped between two unsaved groups lands where it was seen; past 500, which only a full list or a move before the first save leaves, the bottom 500, which land where they show) and asks the tree to reveal `group:<key>` through the generic reveal below. That request also keeps the row mounted through the commit in which the menu closes: a group moved past a tall neighbour would otherwise leave the virtual window, and the menu's focus return would find its ⋯ disconnected.
+- The drag uses pointer events (HTML5 drag and drop never starts from a touch on iOS and cannot scroll a virtualized list). A mouse picks the group up after 4px (`DRAG_START_PX`), touch and pen after a 350 ms long-press (`LONG_PRESS_MS`) without moving more than 8px: a swipe still scrolls, a pen stroke is no drag, a plain click or tap still toggles (pointerdown never prevents anything; "+" and ⋯, other buttons, Ctrl and a second pointer start nothing). Once picked up, the header's own `touchmove` listener, added in `GroupRowView`'s effect with `{ passive: false }` before any touch, cancels the moves so the list holds still (added after touchstart, the browser would already own the gesture; React's `onTouchMove` is passive; on the whole list it would make every scroll wait for the main thread); an uncancelable move ends the drag only if the list really scrolled (Chrome sends some inside its slop). Escape and `contextmenu` are taken over (capture phase, `preventDefault`) only once picked up, so Escape on a mere press still reaches `handleGlobalEscape`; a context menu before that ends the press. A mouse move with `buttons === 0` ends a press whose release was missed; a mouse or pen takes pointer capture when the drag starts. The click after a drop, a cancelled drag or a long-press is eaten (`onClickCapture` on the scroll box, by event time; the next press clears it). Drop targets come from the model (`groupBlocks()`, `groupDropAt()`), never the DOM: rows not mounted, collapsed groups and "show more" count as laid out, a pointer over the pinned section or the footer means the band's first or last place, and a drop where the group already is does nothing. A block runs from its header to the bottom of its last row (sessions, "show more", the empty note), with the spacer heights around it (`gapAbove`, `gapBelow`: 0 next to another group). Groups do not fold up while dragging (the layout would move under the pointer): the block is dimmed in place (its own rows only), a 2px accent line with a 6px dot at its left end marks the drop in the middle of the gap it marks (on the edge between two groups, mid-spacer after the pinned section or before the footer; its `lineY` is at least 3px, so the dot stays whole at the list's top), and a ghost follows the pointer. The ghost is a one-line pill with the project's name (22px high, at most 70% of the tree, ellipsis), shorter than a header so it fits beside the line: on every update `placeGhost()` puts it with `ghostTopFor()` (`lib/session-tree.ts`), pointer first: its bottom 8px (mouse) or 28px (touch, pen: `GHOST_GAP_PX`) above the pointer, and moved only as far as it takes to keep that gap from the pointer, 8px from the line (`GHOST_LINE_GAP_PX`) and inside the list: to the nearest top that does. A line far from the pointer (up to half an expanded block) leaves the pill by the pointer, so it never parks beside the line nor jumps when the target changes; a line just above the pointer puts it above the line; at the list's top it goes below the pointer (and below a line just under it). A line scrolled out of view counts as none; only a list too short to hold the pill clear of both puts it over the pointer, and over the line only when no top in the list keeps clear of it. Horizontally it keeps to the rows' left inset (the header's left edge plus 4px), never under the finger. Its height comes from the CSS (`offsetHeight`), its width from the name. The ghost is a child of `.session-tree`, outside the scroll box: inside it, it would add to the scroll height and auto-scroll would chase it into empty space; auto-scroll (near either edge, up to 14px a frame) also stops at the rows' height. The dragged header stays in `keepMounted`. A drag is also cancelled by rows without its group (archived elsewhere), loading, a hidden tree (archive view, files tab) or a second pointer, and these wait for the release to eat its click; a blur, a hidden page or `pointercancel` end it at once. No `navigator.vibrate` (Chrome warns without a user activation, iOS has none) and no body `user-select` (headers have `user-select: none` and `-webkit-touch-callout: none`).
+- `SessionTree`'s `reveal` (`SessionTreeReveal { id, at, rowKey, takeFocusFrom? }`) is the generic way to bring a row into view after a change put it elsewhere (a fork's copy, a moved group). Ids come from a counter in the parent (`treeRevealIdRef`), never from the last request, which is gone once handled. Until handled the row stays mounted (`keepMounted`). Each look at the rows goes through `revealStep()` (`lib/session-tree.ts`): scrolled to with `revealScrollTop()` (the show-more path's centring) when the row is there, dropped when its row has missed 3 rows updates or the request is more than 3 s old (`at`), found or not, so it never fires later when that row turns up for another reason. Either way the tree calls `onRevealHandled(id)` and the parent clears the request: the search results replace (unmount) the main tree while a query is typed, and a tree mounted again would take a request still held for a new one. Focus moves to the row's first control only when it fell to `<body>` or `takeFocusFrom(active)` agrees; the fork passes "still in the source row, or where closing the archive put it" (`archiveCloseFocusRef`). Only the main tree gets it.
+- Right-click dispatches `pi-web:session-row-contextmenu` first (README, "Downstream Session Context Menu"); only an unclaimed event opens the built-in row menu at the pointer. The ⋯ button opens the built-in menu without dispatching, so there is always a way in. A transient (unsaved) session gets no built-in menu or actions: each would act on a missing file. Renaming or delete-confirming rows ignore right-click.
+- A saved session's title is a real link, `?session=<id>` (`sessionDeepLink()`, `lib/session-links.ts`), so the browser opens it in another tab (#1167). It is only the row's main control (`a.session-tree-main`, styled as the button it replaced), never the whole row: the row's archive and ⋯ buttons are not nested in it. A plain primary click or Enter (`isPlainActivation()`) is the page's: the link stops it from reaching the row, cancels the navigation and selects the session as before (`onSelectFamily`, no page load). Ctrl/Cmd, Shift, Alt and middle-click are the browser's: nothing is cancelled, no `onAuxClick`, no `window.open()`, no `target` (a plain click would leave the tab too). The row's own click handler is unchanged: any click on its empty space, or on a transient row's button, selects (Ctrl/Cmd-click too), as before. The link is relative on purpose: it keeps origin and path (a sub-path deployment) and drops every other parameter and the hash, since a stale `?cwd=` beats `session` in `getInitialNavigation()`; the explicit `session` beats the new tab's remembered one (`withTabOpen()`). A transient session keeps a `<button>`: no other tab could load it. Focus that a reveal or "show more" puts back into a row goes to its first `a[href], button`, the link on a saved row (a bare `button` query found the row's archive button).
+- The row menu offers Open in new tab (T) after the actions that manage the session and just before Delete's separator (after Archive in the group and pinned views, after Fork in the archive view), running or not (`sessionMenuEntries()`). Never first: a menu's first item takes focus when it opens, so Enter right after opening would leave the tab. `openFamilyInNewTab()` calls `window.open(sessionDeepLink(id), "_blank", "noopener,noreferrer")` for the row the menu was opened on, synchronously in the menu's click or key press (an `await` first and the browser blocks it as a popup), and does nothing else: no selection, cwd move or request in this tab. Its return value is not checked: with `noopener` it is null whether a tab opened or not. The new tab loads the session as a reload does, which resumes its wrapper (the event stream's `startRpcSession()`) like any open; nothing creates, copies or prompts a session. An archived session opened that way shows in the chat but has no row in the new tab's main tree (the archive view is closed there), as after any reload. Known limit, not from this change: a link to a session that no longer exists falls back, in a browser profile that already opened a session of that project, to that project's last open session (workspace memory, AppShell's `restoreWorkspaceContext()`), and the URL becomes `/`; in a fresh profile it selects nothing.
+
+New sessions and the files tab ("the files tab" here is the files section below the sessions or the Files tab, whichever the layout shows; the two layouts are the last items below):
+- A group's "+" starts a session at once, with no lookup and no picker: the current project in the sidebar's cwd (its worktree), another project in its root (the main checkout). It passes the group's key and root, so the identity is installed before the cwd moves and a pinned project without sessions keeps its server key. Picking another worktree is the composer bar's job.
+- Every new session goes through `startNewSessionIn()`: it sets the known project identity, moves the sidebar's cwd first (on the click path, like a session pick), then calls `onNewSession(tempId, cwd, projectKey, options)`. AppShell's `handleNewSession` takes that third argument and adopts the project up front (parks the previous project's file tabs, parks a fresh composer's draft only when leaving its cwd). Without it, `handleCwdChange` would take the following cwd report for identity hydration of the fresh composer and keep the previous project's file tabs; letting that path fall through instead would remount the composer and restore the project's last session over it. The header "+" and Ctrl+Alt+N pass no key and keep the current project.
+- File tabs belong to a project (worktrees of one project share them; terminal tabs belong to none). Every project switch (`handleCwdChange`, `handleSelectSession`, `handleNewSession`) goes through `changeFileWorkspace()`: the tabs, the active file and whether the panel showed it are parked by project key in page memory (`switchFileWorkspace()`, `components/file-tab-state.ts`; no file contents, gone on reload) and the target project's come back; an active terminal keeps the panel. Only the active `FileViewer` is mounted, and it reports its mode, wrap and scroll as it unmounts, which is after the switch: the report carries the workspace generation it rendered in (also part of its `key`, so a same-path tab in the next project remounts), and one from an earlier generation goes to the parked tabs (`saveParkedFileViewerState()`), never to a restored tab with the same path. A restored tab whose file is gone shows the viewer's error, as an open tab does.
+- One picker, two places: `components/ProjectWorktreePicker.tsx` is the files' project and worktree boxes (`layout="stacked"` in the Files tab, `"row"` below the sessions: one instance, only the prop follows the layout; both are `stacked` inside, `layout !== "inline"`, with the files' extras: the activity dot, the worktree hint, the menus' minimum width) and the bar above a fresh composer (`layout="inline"`, inside `components/NewSessionContextBar.tsx`, which adds only the focus after a move and the home folder for the paths (the menus', a detached checkout's in the worktree button), asked for once per page). The files' are two boxes, main's files-tab look: the header buttons' grey box, 11px, the project's whole path (`~` for the home folder; no icon, no chevron) and the worktree's branch (branch icon, accent for a linked checkout, "main", the count, a chevron) in code type, cut at their left. The Files tab's are full width, one under the other; below the sessions they sit side by side in one 26px row (`.project-picker.is-row`, app/globals.css: the worktree box as wide as its branch and notes up to 55% of the row, a share that is safe there since the row is the head's whole width; the project box takes the rest, its path cut at its left; the worktree box drops the main checkout's "main" note there, which on a narrow sidebar left the branch no room, the icon's color still telling main from linked; its menu, wider than the box, opens from the box's right edge, `align: "end"`, so it grows over the sidebar rather than past it over the chat). The bar's are text on the page (`.project-picker.is-inline`): no box until hovered or open (`--bg-hover`), 12px, 26px high, 2px apart; a 12px folder icon (`--text-dim`), the project's name in code type and a chevron; the branch icon (accent for a linked checkout), the branch and a chevron, with no "main" note and no count. The name is the menus' (`describeProjectChoices()`): the display name, else the folder's, a dim `parent/` before it only where two projects share it, the parent cut first (`flex-shrink: 1000000`, the "/" never) so the name stays whole; the whole path is the tooltip and the desktop menu's rows. Names there, paths in the files: the bar answers which project the session joins, named as its group header will name it, while the files' boxes stand over the folder the explorer lists. The bar's row never wraps: the worktree button gives way first (`flex-shrink: 1000000`; shrinking with their text, a 30-odd-character branch took the last pixels off a short name at 375px, and at 1000 the name still lost 2/64px, enough for its ellipsis), the branch cut at its left, then the name at its end, down to minimum widths of their own (72px, 56px; the branch capped at 32ch, the name at 28ch), never shares of the row (a lone button squeezed to a share of itself once ended up 0px wide). Both take the sidebar's `NewSessionContext` (the files tab a stand-in with `project: null` and every project while there is no cwd: `ProjectWorktreeContext`), both menus are `SidebarMenu`s (a bottom sheet on phones, keyboard navigation, a filter field from 8 choices on; the field and its "no match" status stand outside the `role="menu"` list, inside a dialog), and the menus offer the same in both places: the projects (the one in use checked, names with a parent folder only where names collide, the full path as tooltip; the files tab adds the group headers' `ActivitySummary` per project and a dot in its box for activity elsewhere), then "Use default directory" and "Open another project…"; the worktrees (the current one checked, "main" noted), then "New worktree…" (`WorktreeCreateForm` as the menu's body). Only the files tab passes `onRemoveWorktree`: each linked checkout gets a trash button (`SidebarMenuItem.secondary`); a dirty checkout or a failure turns the menu into a removal body (Force, focus on Cancel: the body's own on a desktop, the sheet's through `focusCancel` on a phone, never Force as the first control; the menu's `id` drops an answer meant for an older body), a removed one goes back to the list. The picker hands `SidebarMenu` one child, null for a list: two conditional children arrive as an array, which `hasCustomMenuBody()` reads through `Children.toArray` so `[false, false]` is still no body. On a desktop both places' menus look as main's dropdowns did (`classic` on `SidebarMenu`, at least as wide as their box): rows divided by lines, 11px, the projects' whole paths and the branches in code type cut at their left, "Custom path…" for another folder, no line before "New worktree…", whose form shows under the list (`footer`) in its place, a dirty checkout's question in that checkout's row (a `custom` entry; its Cancel goes back to the list) and a failure under the list; the project filter shows past 8 projects, as there (`minChoices`). The phone's sheets keep the names and "Open another project…". Picking the checkout in use does nothing in both places, and so does picking the project in use while its worktrees are listed (it would move a worktree back to the root); without the list (a removed worktree's cwd, grouped under its main project, gets a 403 listing) the project in use is the one-click way back to its root, and the owners ignore a pick of the cwd already in use. Each place keeps its own moves through callbacks: the files tab's `handleFilesPick` installs the picked identity (`setValidatedProject`, as `startNewSessionIn` does, so a pinned project without sessions keeps its server key) and moves the sidebar's cwd (the composer is remounted and its draft parked, as for any cwd change), the bar goes through AppShell as below. Both create through the sidebar's `createWorktree()` and move there only while the form is still open; opening a worktree menu refetches the list. The sessions' "Open another project…" goes to the files (`revealFiles("project-list")`: the files tab, or the section below opened) and opens the picker's project menu through its handle (`ProjectWorktreePickerHandle.openMenu`) once they show.
